## 6. Animation direction — the heart of this brief
6.1 Per-frame drawing law. Every visible frame of the character is an independently constructed vector drawing. Using the previous frame as a starting reference is fine; re-drawing is mandatory — re-positioned construction, re-shaped silhouette, perspective-adjusted proportions on every frame. Never build persistent body-part symbols and tween them. Never morph between two fixed shapes. Never loop a walk or pose cycle.

6.2 Frame budget. 30 s × 24 fps = 720 frames. Fast action (the twirl) animated on ones (24 unique drawings per second); ordinary action on twos (12 per second). Minimum ~300 unique drawings across the film. Any hold longer than 0.5 s must be a moving hold — breathing, weight shift, hair drift, subtly re-drawn each frame.

6.3 Construction and volume. The character exists in 3-D space. Include at least one full-body 3/4 view, one profile or back-3/4 view, and one foreshortened gesture (a hand reaching toward the lens). The anatomy must hold throughout: shoulder/hip counter-tilt when weight rests on one leg; correct occlusion of arms against the torso; feet planted on the floor plane; contact shadows under her in every shot.

6.4 Acting — the classical principles, enforced. Anticipation before every major move (a breath in before a pose flourish, a twist back before the spin). Hands and head move on curved arcs, never straight lines. Hair, ribbon and skirt keep moving 3–6 frames after the body stops, and never move in perfect sync with it. Subtle squash-and-stretch through the spin. Smear frames on the fastest motion in the film. Secondary motion (ribbon, strands, hem) present in every single shot.

6.5 Line and rendering. Variable stroke weight — thicker along silhouette edges, tapered ends, faint hand-drawn wobble / line boil between frames; mathematically perfect uniform Béziers are a failure marker. Painterly shading: soft multi-stop gradients, occlusion in the folds, cast shadows, rim light from the window's key light. Color script across the film: golden-warm entrance → brighter posing middle → soft glow finale.

6.6 Lip sync. In any shot where a character speaks with her mouth visible, mouth shapes alternate per syllable (open / half-open / closed), synchronized to the voice within ±2 frames. If the mouth is off-screen during a line, the body must act to it — a nod, a small gesture.

## 7. Camera — dynamic, virtual-CG-style, cinematic
Minimum 6 distinct shots from at least 4 different camera positions; no shot longer than 8 s; cut on action at least twice. At least 3 shots have camera movement (dolly, pan, orbit) with visible parallax — foreground furniture slides at a different rate than the background wall. Distribute these named setups across the film: establishing wide with slow push-in; low-angle medium; over-the-shoulder; high-angle close-up; dutch tilt for playfulness; tracking arc around the twirl.

## 8. Audio — synthesize everything yourself, no stock assets
Direction per the SUBJECT profile (§3). Use whatever voice-synthesis tool you have; render each line separately, align to the timecodes above, apply light compression, and let the voice sit clearly above the music.

Music: solo piano, serene and cheerful, major key, gentle arpeggiated accompaniment — a small intro, a lift under the twirl, a resolving cadence at 0:28–0:30. Ambience bed: soft room tone, distant birds, very low level. Mix: music ducks ~6 dB under speech; overall loudness around −14 LUFS, true peak ≤ −1 dB. Voice, piano and ambience must all be present and audible in the final MP4's audio track.
